# Part 3 — Create the staging plan for both websites

## Goal

Define the route from your computer to the public website before you begin major development.

Use this structure:

| Environment | Nonprofit | Marketplace | Purpose |
|---|---|---|---|
| Reference | `hopestand-original-reference` | N/A | Study original purchased theme |
| Local development | `hopestand-nonprofit-development` | `hopestand-marketplace-development` | Build safely on your computer |
| Staging | Private host staging site | Private host staging site | Test before publishing |
| Production | `www.hopestand.org` | `marketplace.hopestand.org` | Public live websites |

---

## Step 3.1 — Decide your hosting requirements

Before you choose a host, confirm it can provide:

```text
Two independent WordPress installations
A staging environment for each installation
Automatic backups
Manual backup restoration
SSL certificates
PHP and database support compatible with current WordPress/WooCommerce
SFTP or SSH access
Separate administrator accounts
A clear process for staging-to-production deployment
```

You need two production installations:

```text
www.hopestand.org
marketplace.hopestand.org
```

And two staging installations:

```text
staging.hopestand.org
staging-marketplace.hopestand.org
```

You can also initially use host-provided staging URLs instead of public HopeStand subdomains.

---

## Step 3.2 — Write your deployment flow

Put this workflow in your project documentation:

```text
1. Build locally.
2. Commit code to GitHub.
3. Deploy code to staging.
4. Test staging.
5. Back up production.
6. Deploy approved changes to production.
7. Test production immediately.
8. Document the release and any rollback action.
```

Use this branch structure:

```text
main                 = production-ready code
feature/theme-header = work in progress
feature/woocommerce-cart = work in progress
fix/mobile-menu      = bug fix
```

For example:

```bash
git checkout -b feature/theme-header
```

When the feature is working:

```bash
git add .
git commit -m "Build responsive HopeStand header"
git push -u origin feature/theme-header
```

When you are comfortable with Git, merge tested work into `main`.

---

## Step 3.3 — Set staging rules now

Create these rules before your first staging deployment:

### Staging rules

```text
• Staging must be password protected.
• Staging must be blocked from search-engine indexing.
• Staging must use test payment credentials only.
• Staging must not contain real donor, customer, order, or payment data.
• Staging emails should go only to test/admin inboxes.
• Every production deployment requires a verified backup.
• Every major change must be tested on mobile before deployment.
• Production database changes require a rollback plan.
```

### Production rules

```text
• Never edit theme files directly in production.
• Never test payment changes first in production.
• Never update WordPress, WooCommerce, or major plugins without a backup.
• Never use production customer data in local or staging environments.
• Record the date, change, and result of each production release.
```

